From bc159fdb47fdd338f33c0f51fd56443b016f5126 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: At30c <PabloAtsoc9993@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 21:05:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Fall back to inert BPF maps on legacy kernels

The Linux 4.14 kernel cannot enumerate several of the
/sys/fs/bpf/netd_shared/ maps that BpfNetMaps opens lazily, so
BpfMap.getFirstKey() fails with an ErrnoException and every affected getter
turns it into an IllegalStateException. Because NetworkStatsFactory builds
BpfNetMaps from its own constructor, those unchecked exceptions escape to
SystemServer and loop the device on every boot.

A capture from the installed build shows the failure walking one map at a
time: getLocalNetAccessMap() was made non-fatal first, and the very next boot
then aborted in getLocalNetUidHostAllowlistMap(). Patching a single map only
moves the crash, because all seventeen getters in BpfNetMaps share the same
shape and the same fatal catch handler, so this patches every one of them:
on failure they log and hand back an inert object instead of throwing.

Returning null is not an option, because initBpfMaps(),
updateLocalNetAccessMap() and removeLocalNetAccess() dereference these fields
without a null check. LegacyNoopBpfMap implements IBpfMap with no-op writes
and an empty read side. BpfBoolean gains a constructor that accepts a map, so
it can be backed by that same inert object, which makes get() report false and
set()/clear() do nothing.

Features backed by a map the kernel cannot open therefore degrade instead of
fatal: local network access falls back to the default global policy, tethering
keeps working through netd, and the device boots.

Signed-off-by: At30c <PabloAtsoc9993@outlook.com>
